<Layout title="Latent Dynamics in Adaptive Decision-Making Networks">
  <h1 data-reveal>Understanding Latent Dynamics in Adaptive Decision-Making Networks</h1>
  <p class="entry-meta" data-reveal><span>Research</span><span>In progress</span></p>
  <p class="grey-italics" data-reveal>Research done under the mentorship of Cheng Xue</p>

  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Adaptive behavior often appears simple, but the computations supporting it remain hidden. 
    In this project, I am investigating whether a recurrent neural network trained to solve the same probabilistic foraging task as a macaque develops internal representations analogous to those supporting biological decision-making. 
    More broadly, I wanted to understand which analyses reveal the computations underlying adaptive behavior.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>
    As machine learning models and neural recording technologies become increasingly widespread and powerful, behavior alone is not enough to explain intelligent systems. 
    Two models can produce nearly identical decisions while relying on fundamentally different internal representations, making it difficult to know whether they solve problems in the same way. 
    I believe that developing methods to characterize and compare these latent computations is essential for building interpretable AI, understanding biological intelligence, and ultimately designing trustworthy neurotechnologies.
  </p>

  <h3>Task diagram</h3>
       <Figure src="/images/research/latent-dynamics-adaptive-decision-networks/task_diagram.svg"
               alt="Foraging task structure"
               caption="Task structure: reward contingencies switch on an uncued schedule." />

  <h2>Methods</h2>
  <p>This project investigates how recurrent neural networks develop internal representations while solving a dynamic foraging task inspired by non-human primate experiments. 
  On each trial, the agent chooses between two images with hidden reward probabilities that periodically reverse, requiring continual adaptation to an uncertain environment. 
  Using only the previous action and reward as input, the monkey and network must infer the latent state of the environment and learn a policy that maximizes long-term reward.</p>

  <h3>Task</h3>
  <p>The task is a two-alternative probabilistic decision-making paradigm in which one image is assigned a high reward probability and the other a low reward probability. 
  The identity of the higher-value image reverses unpredictably throughout each session, forcing the agent to integrate reward history over time rather than relying on immediate feedback. 
  This design encourages sequential decision-making under uncertainty and mirrors the behavioral task performed by macaque monkeys.</p>

  <h3>Architecture</h3>
  <p>The agent is implemented as a recurrent neural network with gated recurrent units (GRUs) trained using an actor-critic reinforcement learning framework. 
  At each trial, the network receives the previous action and previous reward as input, updates its hidden state, and produces both an action policy and a value estimate. 
  The hidden state serves as an internal memory, allowing the network to integrate evidence across trials and adapt its behavior as reward contingencies change.</p>

